== Newton–Raphson Method

== The Orbit of $z_0$
#definition-box(outlined: false)[The Orbit of $z_0$][
  For the function $f_c$, defined by $f_c (z)=z^2+c$, and an initial seed $z_0$, the set of iterates given by $z_1=f_c (z_0)$, $z_2=f_c (z_1)$, $...$ is called the _orbit_ of $z_0$ generated by $f_c$.
] <def:orbit>
#set footnote(numbering: n => [])
#footnote[@hmca]

== Julia Set
#definition-box(outlined: false)[Julia set][
  We let $K_c$ denote the set of points with a bounded orbit for $f_c$. The boundary of $K_c$ is known as the _Julia set_ for the function $f_c$.
] <def:julia>
#footnote[@hmca]

== Julia Set Connectedness
#proposition-box(outlined: false)[
  The boundary of $K_c$ is connected if and only if $0 in K_c$. In other words, the Julia set for $f_c$ is connected if and only if the orbit of $0$ is bounded.
]
#footnote[@hmca, Theorem 4.9]
#pagebreak(weak: true)
#set-qed-symbol[Q.E.D.]
#proof[]

== Mandelbrot Set
#definition-box(outlined: false)[Mandelbrot set][
  We let $M$ denote the set of complex numbers $c$ for which the Julia set for $f_c$ is connected, or equivalently, for which the orbit of $0$ under $f_c$ is bounded. The set $M$ is known as the _Mandelbrot set_.
] <def:mandelbrot>
#footnote[@hmca]

== Points
#definition-box(outlined: false)[Fixed point][
  The point $z_0$ is a _fixed point_ for the function $f$ if $f(z_0)=z_0$.
]
#definition-box(outlined: false)[Attracting point][
  The point $z_0$ is an _attracting point_ for the function $f$ if $|f'(z_0)|<1$.
]
#footnote[@hmca, Definition 4.9 and Definition 4.10]

== Local Attraction near an Attracting Fixed Point
#proposition-box(outlined: false)[
  Suppose that $z_0$ is an attracting fixed point for the function $f$. Then there is a disk $D_r (z_0)$ about $z_0$ such that the iterates of all the points in $D_r^*(z_0)$ are drawn toward $z_0$ in the sense that, if $z in D_r^*(z_0)$, then $|f(z)-z_0|<|z-z_0|$. In fact, if $z_k$ is the $k$th iterate of $z in D_r^*(z_0)$, then $limits(lim)_(k -> oo) z_k=z_0$.
]
#footnote[@hmca, Theorem 4.10]
#pagebreak(weak: true)
#proof[]

